### Loans — /loans
#
# Run books.http's "Create a book" and users.http's "Register a reader" first: this file needs both
# ids.
#
# The idempotencyKey in http-client.env.json is deliberately a fixed value, so the second and third
# requests here can demonstrate what happens when a key is repeated.
#
# Change it before each run that should genuinely lend: a key left from a previous run replays that
# run's loan rather than making a new one, which is the mechanism working and is confusing if you
# did not expect it.

### Lend a copy — 201. Stores the loan id.
# The Idempotency-Key header is required, not optional: without it this is a 400.
#
# The copy is taken by a single conditional UPDATE whose WHERE clause carries the rule
# (is_active AND available_copies > 0), and the loan row, the audit entry and the idempotency
# record are committed with it or not at all.
#
# 409 covers: no copies available, the book is withdrawn, and this reader already holds a copy of
# this title — the last enforced by a partial unique index over active loans, not only by a check
# the handler makes.
POST {{host}}/loans
X-Actor: {{actor}}
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "userId": "{{userId}}",
  "loanDays": 14
}

> {%
    if (response.body.data && response.body.data.id) {
        client.global.set("loanId", response.body.data.id);
        client.log("Stored loanId " + response.body.data.id);
    }
%}

### Replay the same Idempotency-Key — 201, same loan, "replayed": true.
# Identical to the request above in every respect, which is the point: same key, same body, so the
# API answers rather than executes. No second loan is created and availability does not move again
# — check with books.http's "Read availability" before and after.
POST {{host}}/loans
X-Actor: {{actor}}
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "userId": "{{userId}}",
  "loanDays": 14
}

### Reuse the key with a different request — 409, and meant to be.
# Same key, but loanDays is 30 rather than 14 — a different request wearing a used key. Replaying
# the first response would tell the caller a loan was made that they did not ask for.
#
# The comparison is a SHA-256 of the request's values rather than of its raw bytes, so reformatting
# the JSON or reordering its properties does not make an honest retry look like this.
POST {{host}}/loans
X-Actor: {{actor}}
Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "userId": "{{userId}}",
  "loanDays": 30
}

### Omit the Idempotency-Key — 400.
# Required by this endpoint, so its absence is an input error like any other.
POST {{host}}/loans
X-Actor: {{actor}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "bookId": "{{bookId}}",
  "userId": "{{userId}}"
}

### Return a copy — 200. The loan is kept and moves to Returned.
# Both halves are conditional updates. The loan moves only if it is still Active, so two concurrent
# returns cannot both succeed — which would otherwise credit the shelf with two copies for one
# loan. Repeating this is a 409 and the shelf is not credited twice.
POST {{host}}/loans/{{loanId}}/return
X-Actor: {{actor}}

### Cancel a loan — 200. The row stays and moves to Cancelled.
# A state deliberately distinct from Returned: a cancelled loan is not one the reader ever
# fulfilled, and the history has to be able to say which of the two happened months later.
#
# The body is optional — a cancellation with no reason is a 200, not a 400. Run after "Return a
# copy" and this answers 409 naming the return, which is the message doing its job.
POST {{host}}/loans/{{loanId}}/cancel
X-Actor: {{actor}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "Reader changed their mind"
}
